    "thm-reparameterization-gradient-under-dominated-differentiation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-variational-autoencoder",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A_X,\\mu_X)$ and $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A_Z,\\mu_Z)$ be sigma-finite measure spaces; that is, each has a countable measurable cover by sets of finite measure. Let $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{d_\\theta}$ and $\\Phi\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{d_\\phi}$ be parameter sets of finite dimension. A latent-variable model with parameter $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ consists of a measurable prior density $r_\\theta(z)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal Z$ and a decoder density $d_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal X$ for each $z$, with\n$$\\int_{\\mathcal Z} r_\\theta(z)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)=1,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathcal X} d_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\,d\\mu_X(x)=1.$$\nThe decoder is jointly measurable in $(x,z)$, and the joint model density is\n$$p_\\theta(x,z)=\\gamma_\\theta(z;x):=r_\\theta(z)d_\\theta(x\\mid z)$$\nrelative to $\\mu_X\\otimes\\mu_Z$. The decoder is also called the generative model: after drawing $z$ from the prior, it assigns a conditional density to the observation $x$.\n\nFor a fixed observation $x$, the evidence density and posterior density are\n$$Z_\\theta(x):=p_\\theta(x):=\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)\\,d\\mu_Z(z),\\qquad \\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x):=\\frac{\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)}{Z_\\theta(x)}.$$\nUse this posterior only when $0<Z_\\theta(x)<\\infty$. A variational encoder is a jointly measurable family of functions $q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal Z$, parameterized by $\\phi\\in\\Phi$ and normalized by\n$$\\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)=1$$\nfor every $(\\phi,x)$; the same parameter $\\phi$ is used for each observation. For an admissible triple $(\\theta,\\phi,x)$ require $0<Z_\\theta(x)<\\infty$ and\n$$q_\\phi(z\\mid x)=0\\quad\\mu_Z\\text{-almost everywhere on }\\{z:\\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x)=0\\},\\qquad \\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)|+|\\log q_\\phi(z\\mid x)|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)<\\infty.$$\nTerms multiplied by $q_\\phi=0$ are defined to be zero. The evidence lower bound is\n$$\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x):=\\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigl(\\log\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)-\\log q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_Z(z).$$\n\nA pathwise representation of the encoder at $x$ is a probability space $(\\mathcal E,\\mathcal F,\\rho)$ whose law $\\rho$ is independent of $\\theta$ and $\\phi$, together with a measurable map $g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x)$ such that the law of $g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x)$ for $\\epsilon\\sim\\rho$ is the measure with density $q_\\phi(\\cdot\\mid x)$ relative to $\\mu_Z$. This representation defines how to generate encoder samples; it does not by itself assert that differentiation may pass through an expectation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-simulation-gradient-objective-and-payoff-oracle",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite dimension $d\\ge1$, an open set $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, and a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$. A simulation payoff is a jointly measurable map $H:\\Theta\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $H(\\theta,\\cdot)$ is integrable for each $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. Its objective is\n$J(\\theta):=\\mathbb E[H(\\theta,\\cdot)].$\nA payoff-oracle call at $\\theta$ returns an integrable real random variable $Y(\\theta)$ with $\\mathbb E[Y(\\theta)]=J(\\theta)$. Two calls may use the same random input or different random inputs; their joint law is part of the chosen coupling. A single oracle call is one function evaluation, regardless of the cost of its internal simulation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-pathwise-simulation-gradient-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use [[def-simulation-gradient-objective-and-payoff-oracle]] and write $J(\\theta)=\\mathbb E[H(\\theta,Z)]$ using one fixed random input $Z$ whose law does not depend on $\\theta$. If, for almost every input, the map $\\theta\\mapsto H(\\theta,Z)$ is differentiable, its pathwise (or reparameterized) gradient estimator is\n$G_{\\mathrm{pw}}(\\theta,Z):=D_\\theta H(\\theta,Z).$\nWhen $H(\\theta,Z)=h(T(\\theta,Z),\\theta)$, with differentiable $T$ and $h$, the chain rule gives\n$G_{\\mathrm{pw}}=D_xh(T(\\theta,Z),\\theta)D_\\theta T(\\theta,Z)+\\partial_\\theta h(T(\\theta,Z),\\theta).$\nThe estimator differentiates the simulated outcome while holding the same random input fixed. This definition alone does not assert that its expectation equals $\\nabla J(\\theta)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-pathwise-simulation-gradient-is-unbiased-under-envelope",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Gradients are Euclidean column vectors, and vector expectations are taken coordinatewise.\n\nLet $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open, let $H:\\Theta\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be the simulation payoff from [[def-simulation-gradient-objective-and-payoff-oracle]], and fix $\\theta_0\\in\\Theta$. Suppose there is an open ball $U$ about $\\theta_0$ such that for almost every $\\omega$, $H(\\cdot,\\omega)$ is continuously differentiable on $U$ and $\\nabla_\\theta H(\\cdot,\\omega)$ is continuous there, and $\\nabla_\\theta H(\\theta,\\cdot)$ is measurable for every $\\theta\\in U$. Assume $\\|\\nabla_\\theta H(\\theta,\\omega)\\|_2\\le M(\\omega)$ for all $\\theta\\in U$ outside one measurable null set, where $M$ is nonnegative and integrable, and assume $\\mathbb E\\|\\nabla_\\theta H(\\theta_0,\\cdot)\\|_2^2<\\infty$. Then $J(\\theta)=\\mathbb E H(\\theta,\\cdot)$ is Fréchet differentiable at $\\theta_0$ and\n$\\nabla J(\\theta_0)=\\mathbb E[\\nabla_\\theta H(\\theta_0,\\cdot)].$\nThus the pathwise estimator from [[def-pathwise-simulation-gradient-estimator]] is unbiased at $\\theta_0$ and has finite coordinate variances.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "fixed-law-payoff-identification",
          "claim": "Fix $i$. By [F3], $G_i(\\eta,\\epsilon):=\\nabla_\\eta H_i(\\eta,\\epsilon)$ is the fixed-noise pathwise estimator. The pushforward condition in [F1] gives $\\mathbb E_\\rho H_i(\\eta,\\epsilon)=\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x_i)$ for every $\\eta$. The absolute log-integrability in the VAE definition makes $H_i$ integrable; its joint measurability and integrability therefore meet the payoff conditions in [F2]. [given, F1, F2, F3]",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "local-dominated-pathwise-transfer",
          "claim": "Since $\\Lambda$ is open, choose a closed ball $K$ centered at $\\eta_0$ with $K\\subset\\Lambda$ and nonempty interior. The common envelope assumed on $K$ is an integrable majorant on an open neighborhood of $\\eta_0$; the C1, continuity, measurability and square-integrability hypotheses are exactly those of [F4]. The full proof of [F4] applies the chain rule along parameter segments and then [F6] to the first-order difference quotients under this majorant. It gives $\\nabla_\\eta\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x_i)\\vert_{\\eta_0}=\\mathbb E_\\rho\\nabla_\\eta H_i(\\eta_0,\\epsilon)$ and finite coordinate variances.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "uniform-finite-data-average",
          "claim": "The finite sum defining $\\overline{\\mathcal L}$ is differentiable at $\\eta_0$ with gradient the average of these $N$ gradients. Independence and uniformity of $I$ give $\\mathbb E[G]=N^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^N\\mathbb E_\\rho\\nabla_\\eta H_i(\\eta_0,\\epsilon)$, which is that average gradient. The finite mixture of the stated square-integrable gradients has finite second moment in each coordinate and therefore finite coordinate variance.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
