        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-variational-autoencoder",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A_X,\\mu_X)$ and $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A_Z,\\mu_Z)$ be sigma-finite measure spaces; that is, each has a countable measurable cover by sets of finite measure. Let $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{d_\\theta}$ and $\\Phi\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{d_\\phi}$ be parameter sets of finite dimension. A latent-variable model with parameter $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ consists of a measurable prior density $r_\\theta(z)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal Z$ and a decoder density $d_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal X$ for each $z$, with\n$$\\int_{\\mathcal Z} r_\\theta(z)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)=1,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathcal X} d_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\,d\\mu_X(x)=1.$$\nThe decoder is jointly measurable in $(x,z)$, and the joint model density is\n$$p_\\theta(x,z)=\\gamma_\\theta(z;x):=r_\\theta(z)d_\\theta(x\\mid z)$$\nrelative to $\\mu_X\\otimes\\mu_Z$. The decoder is also called the generative model: after drawing $z$ from the prior, it assigns a conditional density to the observation $x$.\n\nFor a fixed observation $x$, the evidence density and posterior density are\n$$Z_\\theta(x):=p_\\theta(x):=\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)\\,d\\mu_Z(z),\\qquad \\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x):=\\frac{\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)}{Z_\\theta(x)}.$$\nUse this posterior only when $0<Z_\\theta(x)<\\infty$. A variational encoder is a jointly measurable family of functions $q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal Z$, parameterized by $\\phi\\in\\Phi$ and normalized by\n$$\\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)=1$$\nfor every $(\\phi,x)$; the same parameter $\\phi$ is used for each observation. For an admissible triple $(\\theta,\\phi,x)$ require $0<Z_\\theta(x)<\\infty$ and\n$$q_\\phi(z\\mid x)=0\\quad\\mu_Z\\text{-almost everywhere on }\\{z:\\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x)=0\\},\\qquad \\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)|+|\\log q_\\phi(z\\mid x)|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)<\\infty.$$\nTerms multiplied by $q_\\phi=0$ are defined to be zero. The evidence lower bound is\n$$\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x):=\\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigl(\\log\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)-\\log q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_Z(z).$$\n\nA pathwise representation of the encoder at $x$ is a probability space $(\\mathcal E,\\mathcal F,\\rho)$ whose law $\\rho$ is independent of $\\theta$ and $\\phi$, together with a measurable map $g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x)$ such that the law of $g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x)$ for $\\epsilon\\sim\\rho$ is the measure with density $q_\\phi(\\cdot\\mid x)$ relative to $\\mu_Z$. This representation defines how to generate encoder samples; it does not by itself assert that differentiation may pass through an expectation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-normal-normal-conjugate-update",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $\\mu\\sim N(\\mu_0,\\tau_0^2)$ with $\\tau_0^2>0$, and, conditional\non $\\mu$, let $Y_1,\\ldots,Y_n$ be IID $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with known\n$\\sigma^2>0$ and $n\\ge1$. The sampling data use the Borel sigma-algebra on\n$\\mathbb R^n$ with $n$-dimensional Lebesgue measure. For every\n$y_{1:n}\\in\\mathbb R^n$, define\n$$V_n=(\\tau_0^{-2}+n\\sigma^{-2})^{-1},\\qquad \\mu_n=V_n\\left(\\frac{\\mu_0}{\\tau_0^2} +\\frac{n\\bar y}{\\sigma^2}\\right),\\qquad \\bar y=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^ny_i.$$\nThe evidence is finite and positive, and the posterior is $N(\\mu_n,V_n)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Define $\\gamma(E)=\\int_E e^{-x^2/2}/\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,dx$ for Borel E in $\\mathbb R$. By [[lem-normal-density-has-total-mass-one]] and [[thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure]], gamma is a probability measure; denote it $N(0,1)$. For $m\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, define $N(m,\\sigma^2)$ as the law of $x\\mapsto m+\\sigma x$ on $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B,\\gamma)$. This affine map is continuous: for $\\sigma$>0 choose $\\delta$=$\\varepsilon$/$\\sigma$, and for $\\sigma$=0 it is constant. Its inverse images of opens are open, so it is Borel measurable. [[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]] makes its pushforward a probability. When $\\sigma$=0, the preimage of E is all of R if m belongs to E and empty otherwise, so $N(m,0)=\\delta_m$ in [[def-dirac-measure]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-normal-location-scale-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. For $m\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\sigma>0$, the normal law $N(m,\\sigma^2)$\nof [[def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws]] has Lebesgue density\n$$g_{m,\\sigma}(y)=\\frac1\\sigma\\frac{e^{-((y-m)/\\sigma)^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}=\\frac1{\\sqrt{2\\pi\\sigma^2}}e^{-(y-m)^2/(2\\sigma^2)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-normal-location-scale-mean-and-variance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. For $m\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\sigma\\ge0$, and a real random variable\n$X$ with law $N(m,\\sigma^2)$,\n$$E[X]=m,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sigma^2.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-kullback-leibler-divergence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $P,Q$ be probability measures on the same measurable space\n([[def-probability-measure]]). Absolute continuity $P\\ll Q$ has the convention\nof [[def-absolutely-continuous-with-respect-to-a-positive-measure]]. If\n$P\\not\\ll Q$, define $D(P\\|Q)=+\\infty$. If $P\\ll Q$, assume AC\n([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) as in [[def-likelihood-ratio-as-rn-derivative]] and\nlet $r=dP/dQ$ be a finite-valued nonnegative measurable representative of its\nRadon–Nikodym class; such a representative exists by\n[[lem-radon-nikodym-density-of-a-finite-positive-measure-has-a-nonnegative-representative]].\nOn $\\{r=0\\}$ set $\\ell=0$, and on $\\{r>0\\}$ set $\\ell=\\log r$. This\nfunction is measurable: for $a\\ge0$, $\\{\\ell>a\\}=\\{r>\\exp(a)\\}$, and for\n$a<0$, $\\{\\ell>a\\}=\\{r=0\\}\\cup\\{r>\\exp(a)\\}$, by the inverse and\nmonotonicity properties of the natural logarithm\n([[def-natural-logarithm]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]). These are\nmeasurable sets by [[def-extended-real-valued-measurable-function]]. Since\n$P(\\{r=0\\})=\\int_{\\{r=0\\}}r\\,dQ=0$ by\n[[thm-likelihood-ratio-change-of-measure]], the value chosen there does not\naffect $\\int\\ell\\,dP$. Define\n$$D(P\\|Q):=\\int \\ell\\,dP,$$\nwhere $\\log$ is the natural logarithm ([[def-natural-logarithm]]).\n\nThe definition is independent of the representative: two representatives\nagree $Q$-almost everywhere, hence $P$-almost everywhere. Put\n$$\\psi(0):=0,\\qquad \\psi(t):=t\\log t\\quad(t>0).$$\nThe function $\\psi$ is continuous at zero: for $s\\ge1$,\n$$0\\le \\frac{\\log s}{s}=\\frac1s\\int_1^s\\frac{du}{u}\\le\\frac1s\\int_1^s\\frac{du}{\\sqrt u}\\le\\frac2{\\sqrt s}.$$\nThus $t\\log t\\to0$ as $t\\downarrow0$; here $L=\\log$ by\n[[cor-integral-logarithm-agrees-with-natural-logarithm]] and\n[[def-integral-logarithm]]. On $(0,\\infty)$, $\\psi$ is a product of continuous\nfunctions by [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]] and\n[[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]], so $\\psi$ is continuous on\n$[0,\\infty)$ and $\\psi(r)$ is measurable by\n[[def-extended-real-valued-measurable-function]]. Its signed integral is\ndefined by the positive and negative parts\n([[def-positive-and-negative-parts-of-a-function]],\n[[def-nonnegative-lebesgue-integral]]).\n\nFor $0<t\\le1$, the function $-t\\log t$ has derivative $-\\log t-1$ and\nmaximum $1/e$ at $t=1/e$: $e=\\exp(1)>1$ by\n[[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]] and\n[[thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing]], $1/e=\\exp(-1)$ by\n[[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], and $\\log(1/e)=-1$ by\n[[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]] and the inverse definition. The mean value\ntheorem gives increase before and decrease after this point\n([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-integral-logarithm-derivative-and-normalisation]],\n[[cor-integral-logarithm-agrees-with-natural-logarithm]], [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]).\nAt $t=0$ the negative part is zero. Since $Q$ is a probability measure,\n$$\\int \\psi(r)^-\\,dQ\\le\\frac1e.$$\nThe change-of-measure identity of [[thm-likelihood-ratio-change-of-measure]],\napplied to $\\ell^-$ gives $\\int\\ell^-\\,dP=\\int\\psi(r)^-\\,dQ\\le1/e$,\nso $\\int\\ell\\,dP$ is well-defined; applying it to both positive and negative\nparts gives\n$$D(P\\|Q)=\\int\\ell\\,dP=\\int\\psi(r)\\,dQ=\\int r\\log r\\,dQ,$$\nwith $0\\log0:=0$ and a finite negative part. Therefore the extended integral\nis well-defined without an $\\infty-\\infty$ ambiguity.\n\nThis convention is directional: the first argument must be absolutely\ncontinuous with respect to the second for the integral formula to apply.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-vae-elbo-kl-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix an observation $x$ in a VAE with latent reference measure $\\mu_Z$. Let $\\gamma_\\theta(\\cdot;x)$ be its nonnegative measurable joint-density slice, let $Z_\\theta(x)=\\int\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)\\in(0,\\infty)$, and let $\\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x)=\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)/Z_\\theta(x)$. Let $q_\\phi(\\cdot\\mid x)$ be a measurable probability density satisfying $q_\\phi=0$ $\\mu_Z$-almost everywhere on $\\{\\pi_\\theta=0\\}$ and\n$$\\int q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)|+|\\log q_\\phi(z\\mid x)|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)<\\infty.$$\nThen the choice-free density-ratio identity is\n$$\\log Z_\\theta(x)=\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x)+\\int q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{q_\\phi(z\\mid x)}{\\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x)}\\right)\\,d\\mu_Z(z),$$\nwhere the ratio integrand is zero when $q_\\phi=0$. Under the Axiom of Choice, the final integral is the library reverse-KL divergence $D_{\\rm KL}(q_\\phi\\mu_Z\\|\\pi_\\theta\\mu_Z)$ and is nonnegative; hence $\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x)\\le\\log Z_\\theta(x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "conjugate-posterior",
          "claim": "In the first model set $\\mu_0=0$, $\\tau_0^2=4$, $\\sigma^2=1$, $n=2$, and $\\bar y=(2+4)/2=3$. By [F2], the posterior variance is $(1/4+2)^{-1}=4/9$ and its mean is $(4/9)(0+2\\cdot3)=8/3$; the evidence is finite and positive.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "admissible-positive-gap",
          "claim": "The chosen $q_0=N(0,4/9)$ lies in the family because $0\\in[-1,1]$, while the posterior mean $8/3$ is outside that interval. Both Gaussian densities are strictly positive, so $q_0$ is supported where the posterior is positive. Their logarithms are quadratic and $q_0$ has finite second moment by [F4], so the absolute log-integrability required in [F1] holds. Using the common-variance density formula [F3],\n$$\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{q_0(z\\mid x)}{\\pi(z\\mid x)}\\right)=\\frac{(z-8/3)^2-z^2}{2(4/9)}=-6z+8.$$\nTaking $q_0$-expectations and using [F4] gives $D_{\\rm KL}(q_0\\|\\pi)=8$ by the directional convention [F5].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "elbo-gap-equals-divergence",
          "claim": "Applying [F6] to the first model, the log-evidence minus ELBO is exactly this reverse-KL value $8$, which is strictly positive.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F6",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "collapsed-latent-state",
          "claim": "In the second model, write $h(x)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/2}$ and let $r$ be the $N(0,1)$ prior density. For every $x\\in\\mathbb R$, [F3] gives $0<h(x)<\\infty$ and $\\int r(z)\\,dz=1$, so $\\gamma(z;x)=r(z)h(x)$ has evidence $Z(x)=h(x)$ and posterior $\\pi(z\\mid x)=r(z)$. The constant encoder $q(z\\mid x)=r(z)$ has full support and finite second moment by [F4]; its log density and $\\log\\gamma(z;x)=\\log r(z)+\\log h(x)$ are integrable under $q$ by [F1]. Thus its reverse-KL is zero and [F6] gives ELBO equal to log evidence on every fibre. The decoder density is independent of $z$, so $q(z\\mid x)=p_\\theta(z\\mid x)=p(z)$ for all $x$, which is posterior collapse.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [
        {
          "check": "normal-normal-finite-n-posterior",
          "asserts": "the posterior is $N(8/3,4/9)"
        }
      ],
      "boundaries": [
