      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-58",
        "notes": "Read complete Example and four verification steps with current direct suppliers: VAE, ELBO corollary/AC, directional KL, Normal-Normal posterior proof, normal-law Definition, location-scale density and moments proofs. Recomputed precision 1/4+2=9/4, posterior variance 4/9, mean 8/3; equal-variance log ratio is -6z+8, E_q0 z=0, hence KL=8. Positive Gaussian support and quadratic log gamma/log q plus finite second moment prove admissibility; log gamma=log posterior+log finite evidence. No optimizer or endpoint claim is made. Second model has normalized positive decoder h(x), Z(x)=h(x), and q=posterior=prior for EVERY real x, with finite logs on each fibre; zero KL follows. He et al. v2 §2.2 PDF p.3 checked for all-x condition; only a configuration is asserted, not a training local-optimum or convergence guarantee. Bayes Rules §§5.3.3-5.3.4 and Kingma-Welling §§2.2-3 checked for source conventions. Reader all-fibre repair accepted. Actual item/library census finds no mathematical consumer; only the owned frontmatter-only B page places the item. No diagram or unmet prerequisite."
      }
    },
    "lem-reparameterized-variational-training-finite-run-terminates": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "alg-reparameterized-variational-training",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Inputs are a fixed nonempty finite data set $X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$; a VAE model and admissible encoder from the preceding definition; a parameter-independent base law $\\rho$ and pathwise map $g_\\phi$ for every $x_i$; an initial parameter $\\eta_0=(\\theta_0,\\phi_0)$; a finite positive integer $T$; and positive learning rates $\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{T-1}$. Require the hypotheses of the dominated pathwise-gradient theorem at every parameter value reached by the procedure, and require each update below to remain in its open admissible parameter domain. Let $\\mathcal N$ be the union of the theorem's common null set for finite positive log values and its null set on which the sample-path $C^1$ condition may fail; thus $\\mathcal N$ is a common $\\rho$-null set for all data indices and parameter values.\n\nSet $\\eta_0=(\\theta_0,\\phi_0)$. For each $t=0,\\ldots,T-1$:\n\n1. Draw $I_t$ uniformly from $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ and draw $\\epsilon_t$ from $\\rho$, with $I_t$ and $\\epsilon_t$ independent and the pair $(I_t,\\epsilon_t)$ independent of all earlier random draws.\n2. If $\\epsilon_t\\in\\mathcal N$, set $h_t=0$. Otherwise set $z_t=g_{\\phi_t}(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t})$ and evaluate\n   $$h_t=\\log\\gamma_{\\theta_t}(z_t;x_{I_t})-\\log q_{\\phi_t}(z_t\\mid x_{I_t}).$$\n3. If $\\epsilon_t\\in\\mathcal N$, set $G_t=0$. Otherwise, holding $\\epsilon_t$ and $I_t$ fixed, compute the Euclidean gradient\n   $$G_t=\\nabla_{(\\theta,\\phi)}\\bigl[\\log\\gamma_\\theta(g_\\phi(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t});x_{I_t})-\\log q_\\phi(g_\\phi(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t})\\mid x_{I_t})\\bigr]\\big|_{(\\theta,\\phi)=\\eta_t}.$$\n4. Update $\\eta_{t+1}=\\eta_t+\\alpha_tG_t$.\n\nReturn $(\\eta_0,\\ldots,\\eta_T)$ and $(h_t,G_t)_{0\\le t<T}$. This finite procedure specifies stochastic ascent on the finite-average ELBO; it makes no claim that the iterates converge or reach a global maximum.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-reparameterization-gradient-under-dominated-differentiation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$ be a fixed nonempty finite data set, let $\\Lambda$ be an open subset of a finite-dimensional Euclidean parameter space for $\\eta=(\\theta,\\phi)$, and fix $\\eta_0=(\\theta_0,\\phi_0)\\in\\Lambda$. For each $i$, assume the VAE at $(\\eta,x_i)$ is admissible and has a pathwise representation $g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x_i)$ under one parameter-independent probability law $\\rho$. Assume there is one $\\rho$-null set, common to every $i$ and $\\eta\\in\\Lambda$, outside which $0<q_\\phi(g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x_i)\\mid x_i)<\\infty$ and $0<\\gamma_\\theta(g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x_i);x_i)<\\infty$; define the payoff below to be zero on that null set. Define\n$$H_i(\\eta,\\epsilon):=\\log\\gamma_\\theta(g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x_i);x_i)-\\log q_\\phi(g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x_i)\\mid x_i),$$\nwith terms on a common $\\rho$-null set defined to be zero. Assume each $H_i$ is jointly measurable and integrable for every $\\eta\\in\\Lambda$. Outside one $\\rho$-null set, each $H_i(\\cdot,\\epsilon)$ is continuously differentiable on $\\Lambda$ with continuous gradient, and $\\nabla_\\eta H_i(\\eta,\\cdot)$ is measurable for every $\\eta$. For every compact neighborhood $K\\subset\\Lambda$ of $\\eta_0$, assume one nonnegative $M_{i,K}\\in L^1(\\rho)$ satisfies $\\|\\nabla_\\eta H_i(\\eta,\\epsilon)\\|_2\\le M_{i,K}(\\epsilon)$ for every $\\eta\\in K$ outside one common null set, and assume $\\mathbb E_\\rho\\|\\nabla_\\eta H_i(\\eta_0,\\epsilon)\\|_2^2<\\infty$.\n\nLet $I$ be uniform on $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ and independent of $\\epsilon\\sim\\rho$. Then the finite-average objective\n$$\\overline{\\mathcal L}(\\eta):=\\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^N\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x_i)$$\nis differentiable at $\\eta_0$, and its one-example pathwise gradient estimate\n$$G(\\eta_0,I,\\epsilon):=\\nabla_\\eta H_I(\\eta_0,\\epsilon)$$\nsatisfies $\\mathbb E[G(\\eta_0,I,\\epsilon)]=\\nabla_\\eta\\overline{\\mathcal L}(\\eta_0)$ and has finite coordinate variances. A parameter-independent Gaussian base law alone does not imply the differentiability, envelope, or second-moment hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-reparameterized-variational-training",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Inputs are a fixed nonempty finite data set $X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$; a VAE model and admissible encoder from the preceding definition; a parameter-independent base law $\\rho$ and pathwise map $g_\\phi$ for every $x_i$; an initial parameter $\\eta_0=(\\theta_0,\\phi_0)$; a finite positive integer $T$; and positive learning rates $\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{T-1}$. Require the hypotheses of the dominated pathwise-gradient theorem at every parameter value reached by the procedure, and require each update below to remain in its open admissible parameter domain. Let $\\mathcal N$ be the union of the theorem's common null set for finite positive log values and its null set on which the sample-path $C^1$ condition may fail; thus $\\mathcal N$ is a common $\\rho$-null set for all data indices and parameter values.\n\nSet $\\eta_0=(\\theta_0,\\phi_0)$. For each $t=0,\\ldots,T-1$:\n\n1. Draw $I_t$ uniformly from $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ and draw $\\epsilon_t$ from $\\rho$, with $I_t$ and $\\epsilon_t$ independent and the pair $(I_t,\\epsilon_t)$ independent of all earlier random draws.\n2. If $\\epsilon_t\\in\\mathcal N$, set $h_t=0$. Otherwise set $z_t=g_{\\phi_t}(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t})$ and evaluate\n   $$h_t=\\log\\gamma_{\\theta_t}(z_t;x_{I_t})-\\log q_{\\phi_t}(z_t\\mid x_{I_t}).$$\n3. If $\\epsilon_t\\in\\mathcal N$, set $G_t=0$. Otherwise, holding $\\epsilon_t$ and $I_t$ fixed, compute the Euclidean gradient\n   $$G_t=\\nabla_{(\\theta,\\phi)}\\bigl[\\log\\gamma_\\theta(g_\\phi(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t});x_{I_t})-\\log q_\\phi(g_\\phi(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t})\\mid x_{I_t})\\bigr]\\big|_{(\\theta,\\phi)=\\eta_t}.$$\n4. Update $\\eta_{t+1}=\\eta_t+\\alpha_tG_t$.\n\nReturn $(\\eta_0,\\ldots,\\eta_T)$ and $(h_t,G_t)_{0\\le t<T}$. This finite procedure specifies stochastic ascent on the finite-average ELBO; it makes no claim that the iterates converge or reach a global maximum.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "alg-reparameterized-variational-training",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Inputs are a fixed nonempty finite data set $X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$; a VAE model and admissible encoder from the preceding definition; a parameter-independent base law $\\rho$ and pathwise map $g_\\phi$ for every $x_i$; an initial parameter $\\eta_0=(\\theta_0,\\phi_0)$; a finite positive integer $T$; and positive learning rates $\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{T-1}$. Require the hypotheses of the dominated pathwise-gradient theorem at every parameter value reached by the procedure, and require each update below to remain in its open admissible parameter domain. Let $\\mathcal N$ be the union of the theorem's common null set for finite positive log values and its null set on which the sample-path $C^1$ condition may fail; thus $\\mathcal N$ is a common $\\rho$-null set for all data indices and parameter values.\n\nSet $\\eta_0=(\\theta_0,\\phi_0)$. For each $t=0,\\ldots,T-1$:\n\n1. Draw $I_t$ uniformly from $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ and draw $\\epsilon_t$ from $\\rho$, with $I_t$ and $\\epsilon_t$ independent and the pair $(I_t,\\epsilon_t)$ independent of all earlier random draws.\n2. If $\\epsilon_t\\in\\mathcal N$, set $h_t=0$. Otherwise set $z_t=g_{\\phi_t}(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t})$ and evaluate\n   $$h_t=\\log\\gamma_{\\theta_t}(z_t;x_{I_t})-\\log q_{\\phi_t}(z_t\\mid x_{I_t}).$$\n3. If $\\epsilon_t\\in\\mathcal N$, set $G_t=0$. Otherwise, holding $\\epsilon_t$ and $I_t$ fixed, compute the Euclidean gradient\n   $$G_t=\\nabla_{(\\theta,\\phi)}\\bigl[\\log\\gamma_\\theta(g_\\phi(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t});x_{I_t})-\\log q_\\phi(g_\\phi(\\epsilon_t,x_{I_t})\\mid x_{I_t})\\bigr]\\big|_{(\\theta,\\phi)=\\eta_t}.$$\n4. Update $\\eta_{t+1}=\\eta_t+\\alpha_tG_t$.\n\nReturn $(\\eta_0,\\ldots,\\eta_T)$ and $(h_t,G_t)_{0\\le t<T}$. This finite procedure specifies stochastic ascent on the finite-average ELBO; it makes no claim that the iterates converge or reach a global maximum.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-variational-autoencoder",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A_X,\\mu_X)$ and $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A_Z,\\mu_Z)$ be sigma-finite measure spaces; that is, each has a countable measurable cover by sets of finite measure. Let $\\Theta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{d_\\theta}$ and $\\Phi\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{d_\\phi}$ be parameter sets of finite dimension. A latent-variable model with parameter $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ consists of a measurable prior density $r_\\theta(z)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal Z$ and a decoder density $d_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal X$ for each $z$, with\n$$\\int_{\\mathcal Z} r_\\theta(z)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)=1,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathcal X} d_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\,d\\mu_X(x)=1.$$\nThe decoder is jointly measurable in $(x,z)$, and the joint model density is\n$$p_\\theta(x,z)=\\gamma_\\theta(z;x):=r_\\theta(z)d_\\theta(x\\mid z)$$\nrelative to $\\mu_X\\otimes\\mu_Z$. The decoder is also called the generative model: after drawing $z$ from the prior, it assigns a conditional density to the observation $x$.\n\nFor a fixed observation $x$, the evidence density and posterior density are\n$$Z_\\theta(x):=p_\\theta(x):=\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)\\,d\\mu_Z(z),\\qquad \\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x):=\\frac{\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)}{Z_\\theta(x)}.$$\nUse this posterior only when $0<Z_\\theta(x)<\\infty$. A variational encoder is a jointly measurable family of functions $q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\ge0$ on $\\mathcal Z$, parameterized by $\\phi\\in\\Phi$ and normalized by\n$$\\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)=1$$\nfor every $(\\phi,x)$; the same parameter $\\phi$ is used for each observation. For an admissible triple $(\\theta,\\phi,x)$ require $0<Z_\\theta(x)<\\infty$ and\n$$q_\\phi(z\\mid x)=0\\quad\\mu_Z\\text{-almost everywhere on }\\{z:\\pi_\\theta(z\\mid x)=0\\},\\qquad \\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)|+|\\log q_\\phi(z\\mid x)|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_Z(z)<\\infty.$$\nTerms multiplied by $q_\\phi=0$ are defined to be zero. The evidence lower bound is\n$$\\operatorname{ELBO}_{\\theta,\\phi}(x):=\\int_{\\mathcal Z}q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigl(\\log\\gamma_\\theta(z;x)-\\log q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_Z(z).$$\n\nA pathwise representation of the encoder at $x$ is a probability space $(\\mathcal E,\\mathcal F,\\rho)$ whose law $\\rho$ is independent of $\\theta$ and $\\phi$, together with a measurable map $g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x)$ such that the law of $g_\\phi(\\epsilon,x)$ for $\\epsilon\\sim\\rho$ is the measure with density $q_\\phi(\\cdot\\mid x)$ relative to $\\mu_Z$. This representation defines how to generate encoder samples; it does not by itself assert that differentiation may pass through an expectation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-natural-numbers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**\n(Inductive set),\n\n$$\\mathbb{N} = \\omega := \\bigcap\\,\\{\\, I : I \\text{ is inductive} \\,\\},$$\n\nwhich exists and is itself inductive by The natural numbers exist: a smallest inductive set (the\nAxiom of Infinity, The Axiom of Infinity: there is a set containing a set with no elements and closed under $y \\mapsto y \\cup \\{y\\}$, supplies one inductive set to\nintersect within, and Separation, The Axiom Schema of Separation: for each formula $\\varphi$, $\\forall \\bar p\\,\\forall x\\,\\exists y\\,\\forall z\\,(z \\in y \\leftrightarrow (z \\in x \\wedge \\varphi(z,\\bar p)))$, makes the\nintersection a set). On $\\mathbb{N}$ we take\n\n$$0 := \\varnothing, \\qquad \\sigma(n) := n \\cup \\{n\\},$$\n\nthe distinguished element and the successor function. Thus\n$0 = \\varnothing$, $1 = \\{0\\}$, $2 = \\{0, 1\\}$, $3 = \\{0, 1, 2\\}$, and in general\n$n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ is the set of its predecessors.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-induction-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$. If $0 \\in S$ and $\\sigma(n) \\in S$ whenever $n \\in S$, then $S = \\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently (property form): if a property $P$ satisfies $P(0)$ and ($P(n) \\Rightarrow P(\\sigma(n))$) for all $n$, then $P(n)$ holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. This is the induction principle, the basis of proof by induction.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
